This paper examines the existence of solutions to the following singular double phase problem defined on a bounded open subset $\Omega$ of a complete, non-negatively curved manifold $M$:
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta u = u^{-\gamma} + |u|^{q-2}u & \text{in } \Omega, \\
u > 0 & \text{in } \Omega, \\
u=0 & \text{on } \partial\Omega,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $u$ is the unknown function, $\Delta$ denotes the Laplace-Beltrami operator, and $\gamma > 0$ is a constant. The associated differential operator $-\Delta$ features a variable exponent $p(x)$ that may vanish, while the growth exponent $q$ is continuous. Our analysis covers scenarios where the ratio $q/p(x)$ exceeds or equals the critical Sobolev exponent, as well as cases involving singularities.

The density-gradient-corrected correlation function (denoted as $\xi_{
ablaho}$) is an emerging statistical tool for quantifying the clustering of dark matter and galaxies. This metric measures the probability of finding a particle at a specific location relative to a reference particle, normalized by the average matter density. In this study, we investigate the properties of $\xi_{
ablaho}$ within the context of the $\Lambda$CDM framework. We demonstrate that the function's shape is largely independent of the specific galaxy tracer selection, provided the selection bias is smooth. Furthermore, we examine the potential of $\xi_{
ablaho}$ as a probe for cosmological parameters, specifically the matter density and the amplitude of initial density fluctuations. We find that this function offers superior constraints on both the matter density and the amplitude of initial density fluctuations compared to the standard correlation function.
